 ],
 "literal": "Two-knowing thief, our beloved! / With strength-having might's red-speared Malaiyaṉ's / Mullūr's forest smelling, having come, / in the dense-sounding night, like our people she is. / Tresses-adorned mixed flowers having shed, / sandalwood-stroked fragrant tresses oil having smeared, / unloving-faced having become, / like her own people she is, at dawn indeed!",
 "translation": "My beloved is a thief who knows two different ways! In the pitch-dark midnight, she comes to me smelling of the wild forests of Mullūr, belonging to Malaiyaṉ of the fierce red spear, and she is so intimate, just like one of our own. But at dawn, she shakes off the mixed flowers that adorned her hair, rubs oil into her fragrant tresses dried with sandalwood, and with a cold, unloving face, she becomes distant, just like one of her own family.",
 "modern": "இரண்டு விதமான தன்மைகளை அறிந்த கள்வி என் காதலி ஆவாள். மாறுபாடு கொண்ட வலிமையையும் சிவந்த வேலையும் உடைய மலையனது முள்ளூர் காட்டு நறுமணத்துடன் நள்ளிரவில் நம்மிடம் வந்து நம்மைப் போன்றவளாக நெருங்கிப் பழகுகிறாள். ஆனால், விடியற்காலையிலோ, தன் கூந்தலில் சூடியிருந்த பலவகை மலர்களை உதிர்த்துவிட்டு, சந்தனம் பூசி உலர்த்திய நறிய கூந்தலில் எண்ணெயைத் தடவிக்கொண்டு, விருப்பமில்லாத முகத்தோடு தன் சுற்றத்தாரைப் போல அந்நியமாகி விடுகிறாள்.",
 "explanation": "The speaker is the hero, talking to himself about his beloved's contrasting behavior. He describes her as a 'thief who knows two ways' because she acts completely differently depending on the time of day. During their secret tryst in the dead of night, she comes to him smelling of the wild forests of Mullūr, belonging to the chieftain Malaiyaṉ, and behaves with complete intimacy and love. However, as dawn approaches, she sheds her tryst-flowers, oils her hair, and adopts a cold, distant expression to blend back in with her family. This contrast highlights the secrecy and anxiety of their love in the Kuriñci landscape, where the beloved must hide her passion from her kin.",
